{"answer":"We keep each fisher’s permit in a small wooden box on the dock. Every time I finish a trip I hand my permit to the community leader, who records my weight in the ledger. If the ledger shows I’ve gone over the limits, the leader takes my card from the box and keeps it for the next day – I simply don’t get a card back until the following day. That’s the one‑trip ban: the permit is withheld until the leader returns it. The leader holds all permits in the box; he removes a fisher’s card by taking it and not handing it back until the ban period ends. This way everyone knows who’s banned and for how long.

We all understand that the permit is the key to fishing: without it I cannot launch my net or paddle out. The leader’s removal of the card is the enforcement. That is why the community agrees to stick to the rules.

(If any confusion about the ban remains, we can add a simple “BANNED” sticker on the card when it’s taken, so it’s clear it’s in the leader’s custody.)","revised_policy":"Each fisher may catch no more than 20 kg per trip and no more than 100 kg per week. Fisheries will keep a simple paper ledger. After each trip the fisher writes name, date and weight caught. At the end of the week the community leader tallies totals. If a fisher exceeds the weekly limit, they must return the excess to the lake or donate it to the communal pot. Violations also incur a one‑trip fishing ban. All fish above the 20 kg per‑trip limit must be left in the lake for a day before the next trip.","revised_operationalization":"The community leader receives the ledger each trip, records the entries, and on Sunday reviews the totals. If a fisher has exceeded limits, the leader informs them to return or donate the excess and applies a one‑trip ban by removing their fishing permit for the next day. The leader also checks that any fish above 20 kg were left in the lake for at least one day before the next trip.","reasoning":"I kept the policy and operationalization unchanged because the question only asked how the ban is enforced. I clarified that the leader holds the permits in a box and withholds them to enforce the ban, which answers the question while staying true to the original rules."}

